Q: Connecting to database with Jetty and JNDI/JPA A: <p>You have to add a prefix to find the JNDI resource. For example, to find your resource:</p>
<pre><code>
&lt;New id=&quot;testData&quot; class=&quot;org.eclipse.jetty.plus.jndi.Resource&quot;&gt;
    &lt;Arg/&gt;
    &lt;Arg&gt;testData&lt;/Arg&gt;
    &lt;Arg&gt;
        ...
    &lt;/Arg&gt;
&lt;/New&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>You need to search the server for it like this:</p>
<pre><code>InitialContext ctx = new InitialContext();
DataSource myoracledb = (DataSource)ctx.lookup(&quot;java:comp/env/testData&quot;);
</code></pre>
<p>EDIT: Also, you could try adding a reference to the server to your JNDI datasource like this:</p>
<pre><code> &lt;New id=&quot;testData&quot; class=&quot;org.eclipse.jetty.plus.jndi.Resource&quot;&gt;
    &lt;Arg&gt;&lt;Ref refid=&quot;Server&quot;/&gt;&lt;/Arg&gt;
    &lt;Arg&gt;testData&lt;/Arg&gt;
    &lt;Arg&gt;
        ...
    &lt;/Arg&gt;
&lt;/New&gt;
</code></pre>

Q: Rails equivalent to laravel&#39;s &quot;$loop-&gt;first&quot; A: <p>In Rails ERB templates, you'd use <code>each_with_index</code> or <code>each.with_index</code> to track position:</p>
<pre><code>&lt;% @items.each_with_index do |item, index| %&gt;
  &lt;% if index == 0 %&gt;
    &lt;!-- Make the first item look different --&gt;
  &lt;% else %&gt;
    &lt;!-- render the rest of the items as a list --&gt;
  &lt;% end %&gt;
&lt;% end %&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>Or you can use <code>each_with_object</code> / Ruby's built-in <code>first</code> if you just need to separate the first item from the rest before looping:</p>
<pre><code>&lt;% @items.first.tap do |item| %&gt;
  &lt;!-- featured item --&gt;
&lt;% end %&gt;

&lt;% @items.drop(1).each do |item| %&gt;
  &lt;!-- rest as a list --&gt;
&lt;% end %&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>The second approach is often cleaner because it avoids the conditional inside the loop entirely — you just handle the first item separately, then iterate over the remainder. This maps pretty naturally to the &quot;hero + grid&quot; UI pattern you described.</p>
<p>If you're using ViewComponents or partials, you might also see this pattern:</p>
<pre><code>&lt;%= render FeaturedComponent.new(item: @items.first) %&gt;
&lt;%= render partial: &quot;item&quot;, collection: @items.drop(1) %&gt;
</code></pre>
